Device Selection & Justification

Modem: Translates the external ISP signal into a usable digital format; mandatory for internet access.

Router: Connects directly to the modem to assign IP addresses, manage NAT, and direct traffic between the internal network and the internet.

Switch: Deployed per floor or department. Operates at Layer 2 to route data via MAC addresses directly to the intended device, eliminating collision domains and ensuring dedicated bandwidth.

Repeater: Placed at signal degradation points to amplify and extend network range through heavy factory walls or past the standard 100-meter Ethernet limit.

Hub: Never use this. It is obsolete hardware that blindly broadcasts data to all ports, crippling performance and destroying network security.

Data Flow Mechanics

Internal Traffic: A file sent between departments travels from the PC to the local Switch, then directly to the destination Switch and target PC. The Router is bypassed entirely for local transfers.

External Traffic: Web requests travel from the PC to the Switch, up to the Router for IP translation, out through the Modem, and finally to the ISP.

Edge Connections: A remote machine sends a weak signal to the Repeater, which regenerates the packet at full strength and passes it down the line to the main network.
